\id 1PE Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 1 Peter
\toc1 The First Epistle of Peter the Apostle.
\toc2 1 Peter
\toc3 1PE
\mt1 The First Epistle of Peter the Apostle.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF BOTH THE EPISTLES OF S. PETER, THE FIRST, AND THE SECOND.
\ip OF S. Peter we read at large, both in the Gospels, and in the Acts of the Apostles: and namely, that Christ designed him, and also made him his vicar (as S. Matthew \f - \ft \+it c. 10. v. 2.\+it*\f* for that cause in the catalogue of the Apostles, called him \it Primus, the first\it* , and all antiquity, \it Princeps Apostolorum, the Prince of the Apostles\it* ) and that he accordingly executed that office after Christ's departure, planting the Church first among the Jews in Hierusalem and in all that country and coasts about, as Christ also himself before had preached to the Jews alone.
\ip But preaching at length to the Gentiles also, according to Christ's commission (Mat. 28. v. 19.) and being now come to Rome, the head city of the Gentiles, from thence he writeth this Epistle to his Christian Jews, having care of them in his absence, no less than when he was present: and not to the Jews that were at home, (belike because they had S. James, or his successor S. Simon Cleophae, resident with them) but \f - \ft \+it 1. Pet. 1.\+it*\f* to them that were dispersed in Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, and Bithynia.
\ip And that he writeth it from Rome, himself signifieth saying: \f - \ft \+it 1. Pet. 5.\+it*\f* \it The Church that is in Babylon saluteth you\it* . \f - \ft * See the Annotation \+it 1. Pet. 5. v. 13.\+it*\f* Where by Babylon he meaneth Rome, as all antiquity doth interpret him: not, that he so calleth the Church of Rome, but the heathen state of the Roman empire, which then, and 300 years after, unto the conversion of Constantinus the Emperor, did persecute the elect Church of Rome, in so much that the first 33 Bishops thereof unto S. Silvester, were all Martyrs.
\ip For the matter whereof he writeth, himself doth signify it in these words: \it This lo the second Epistle I write to you, my dearest, in Which (Epistles\it* \f - \ft \+it 2. Pet. 3.\+it*\f* \it ) I stir up by admonition, your sincere mind, that you may be mindful of those words &c.\it* So he saith there of both together. And again of the first to the same purpose, in another place: \f - \ft \+it 1. Pet. 5.\+it*\f* \it I have briefly written, beseeching and testifying that this is the true grace of God, wherein you stand\it* . For, there were at that time certain Seducers (as \f - \ft * See the Annotation upon S. James' epistle \+it c. 2. v. 21.\+it*\f* S. August. also hath told us) who went about to teach \it Only faith\it* , as though good works were not necessary, nor meritorious. There were also great persecutions, to compel them with terror to deny Christ & all his religion. He therefore exhorteth them accordingly, neither for persecution, neither by seduction to forsake it: though in the first, his exhortation is more principally against persecution: and in the second, more principally against seduction. The first epistle is noted to be very like to S. Paul's epistle to the Ephesians, in words also, and so thick of Scriptures, as though he spake nothing else.
\ip The time when the first was written, is uncertain: the second was written a little before his death, as is gathered by his words in the same. c. 1. v. 14.
\c 1
\cd He comforteth them in their persecutions (being now by Baptism made the children of God) with the hope of their heavenly inheritance: 6. shewing how meritorious it is for them to be so constant in faith; 10. and confirming them therein with the authority of the Prophets and of the Holy Ghost. 15. Exhorting them to live also accordingly in all holiness, 15. considering the holiness of God, the uprightness of his judgement, the price of their redemption by Christ, 22. and the virtue of the seed in them, (which is grace regenerative in Baptism) foretold by the Prophet also.
\p
\v 1 \sc Peter\sc* \f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft The Epistle \+it In Cathedra S. Petri Rome.\+it* Ian. 18. \+it & Antiochiæ\+it* Feb. 22.\f* an Apostle of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, to the elect strangers of the dispersion of Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia,
\v 2 according to the prescience of God the Father, into sanctification of the Spirit, unto the obedience and sprinkling of the blood of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ: Grace to you and peace be multiplied.
\v 3 \x - \xt 2. Cor. 1, 3. Eph. 1, 3.\x*\f 2 \fr 1.3 \ft The Epistle for many martyrs.\f* Blessed be God and the Father of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, who according to his great mercy hath regenerated us unto a lively hope, by the resurrection of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ from the dead,
\v 4 unto an inheritance incorruptible, and incontaminate, and that cannot fade, conserved in the heavens in you,
\v 5 (who in the virtue of God are kept by faith unto salvation) ready to be revealed in the last time.
\v 6 Wherein you shall rejoice, a little now if you must be made heavy in diverse tentations:
\v 7 that the probation of your faith much more precious than gold (which is proved by the fire) may be found unto praise and glory and honour in the revelation of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ:
\v 8 whom having not seen, you love: in whom now also not seeing you believe: and believing you rejoice with joy unspeakable and glorified,
\v 9 receiving the end of your faith, the salvation of your souls.
\v 10 Of the which salvation the Prophets inquired & searched, which prophesied of the grace to come in you,
\v 11 searching unto which or what manner of time the Spirit of Christ in them did signify: fore-telling those passions that are in Christ, and the glories following:
\v 12 to whom it was revealed, that not to themselves, but to you they ministered those things which now are told you by them that have evangelized to you, the Holy Ghost being sent from Heaven, on whom the Angels desire to look.
\v 13 For the which cause having the loins of your \f 1 \fr 1.13 \ft Chastity not only of body but also of mind, is required. \+it S. Bede upon this place.\+it*\f* mind girded, sober, trust perfectly in that grace which is offered you, in the revelation of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ,
\v 14 as children of obedience, not configurated to the former desires of your ignorance:
\v 15 but according to him that hath called you, the Holy one, be you also in all conversation holy:
\v 16 because it is written: \it You shall be holy, because I am holy.\it* \x - \xt Lev. 11, 19. 20, 7. Deu. 10. Ro. 2. Gal. 2.\x*
\v 17 And if you invocate the Father, him which without acception of persons judgeth according to every one's \f 1 \fr 1.17 \ft c God will judge men according to every one's works, and not by faith only.\f* work; in fear converse ye the time of your sojourning.
\v 18 Knowing that \x - \xt 1. Cor. 6, 20. 7, 23.\x* not with corruptible things, gold or silver, you are redeemed from your vain conversation of your fathers' \f 2 \fr 1.18 \ft He meaneth the errors of Gentility. Or if he write to the Jews dispersed, he meaneth the yoke of the Law with the fond and heavy additions of their later Masters called \+it Deuteroses.\+it* The Heretics, to make it sound to the simple against the traditions of the Church, corrupt the text thus, \+it which you have received by tradition of the Fathers.\+it*\f* tradition:
\v 19 but with the precious blood as it were of an immaculate and unspotted Lamb, Christ,
\v 20 \x - \xt Ro. 16, 25. Col. 1, 26. Tit. 1, 2.\x* foreknown indeed before the constitution of the world, but manifested in the last times for you,
\v 21 which by him are faithful in God who raised him from the dead, & hath given him glory, that your faith and hope might be in God.
\v 22 Making your souls chaste in obedience of charity, in the sincere love of the fraternity from the heart love ye one another earnestly:
\v 23 born again not of corruptible seed, but incorruptible by the word of God who liveth & remaineth for ever.
\v 24 For \x - \xt Esa. 40, 6.\x* \it all flesh is as grass: & all the glory thereof as the flower of grass. The grass is withered, and the flower thereof is fallen away.\it*
\v 25 But the word of our Lord remaineth for ever, and this is the word that is evangelized among you.
\c 2
\cd Now after their Baptism, what must be their meat: 4. and being come to Christ, how happy they be above their incredulous brethren, according to the Scriptures also. 11. Whereupon he beseecheth them to shine in good life among the Heathen, so to procure their conversion: 13. to be obedient subjects to higher Powers (how soever some misconster Christian liberty) 14. and servants to obey their Masters. 19. And so, doing well, though they suffer for it, it is very meritorious. 21. whereas Christ also not only gave them example, 24. but also by his death hath made them able to live justly.
\p
\v 1 \sc Laying\sc* \f 1 \fr 2.1 \ft The Epistle upon Sunday in Easter week.\f* away therefore all malice, and all guile, and simulations, and envies, and all detractions,
\v 2 as infants even now born, reasonable, milk without guile desire ye, that in it you may grow unto salvation.
\v 3 If yet you have tasted that our Lord is sweet.
\v 4 Unto whom approaching, a living stone, of men indeed reprobated, but of God elect and made honourable:
\v 5 be ye also yourselves super-edified as it were living stones, \f 1 \fr 2.5 \fq spiritual houses \ft \+it a spiritual house,\+it*\f* spiritual houses, a holy priesthood, to offer spiritual hosts, acceptable to God by \sc Jesus\sc* Christ. \ef - \fr 2.5 \fq Spiritual hosts. \ft ¹ Here we see, that as he speaketh of spiritual hosts, which every Christian man offereth, so he speaketh not properly of priesthood, when he maketh all Priests, but of a spiritual priesthood. Which spiritual priesthood was also in all the Jews: but the priesthood (properly so called) was only in the sons of Aaron, and they offered the sacrifices (properly so called) which none besides might offer. \fq ¹ \ft Spiritual hosts and Priests.\ef*
\v 6 For the which cause the Scripture containeth, \x - \xt Esa. 28, 16.\x* \it Behold I put in Sion a principal corner-stone elect, precious. And he that shall believe in him, shall not be confounded.\it*
\v 7 To you therefore that believe, honour: but to them that believe not, \x - \xt Ps. 117. Mt. 21. Act. 4. Esa. 8. Ro. 9, 33. Exo. 19. Apoc. 1.\x* \it the stone which the builders rejected, the same is made into the head of the corner:\it*
\v 8 and a stone of offence, & a rock of scandal, to them that stumble at the word, neither do believe \f 1 \fr 2.8 \fq wherein also they are put \ft \+it whereto also they are ordained.\+it*\f* wherein also they are put.
\v 9 But you are an elect generation, a \f 1 \fr 2.9 \ft c The Protestants can no more gather of this, that all Christians be Priests: than, that all be kings: as is most plain \+it Apoc. 1, 6. & 5, 10. Thou hast made us a Kingdom (or Kings) & Priests.\+it*\f* kingly priesthood, a holy nation, a people of purchase: that you may declare his virtues, which from darkness hath called you into his marvelous light.
\v 10 \x - \xt Ose. 2. Ro. 9.\x* \it Which sometime not a people: but now the people of God. Which not having obtained mercy: but now having obtained mercy.\it*
\v 11 \x - \xt Gal. 5, 16.\x* My dearest I beseech you as strangers & pilgrims, \f 2 \fr 2.11 \ft The Epistle upon the 3. Sunday after Easter.\f* to refrain yourselves from carnal desires which war against the soul,
\v 12 having your conversation good among the Gentiles: that in that wherein they misreport of you as of malefactors, by the good works considering you, \x - \xt Mt. 5, 16.\x* they may glorify God in the day of visitation.
\v 13 \x - \xt Ro. 13, 1.\x* Be subject therefore to every \f 2 \fr 2.13 \ft So is the Greek, but the Protest. in favour of temporal laws made against the Cat. religion, translate it very falsely thus, \+it to all manner ordinance of man:\+it* themselves boldly rejecting Ecclesiastical decrees as men's ordinances.\f* human \f 3 \fr 2.13 \ft \+it c κτίσει.\+it*\f* creature for God: whether it be to king, as excelling: \ef - \fr 2.13 \fq Be subject. \ft ¹ Not only our Master Christ, but the Apostles and all Christians were ever charged by such as thought to bring them in hatred with Princes, with disobedience to kings and temporal Magistrates, therefore both ² S. Paul and this Apostle do specially warn the faithful, that they give no occasion by their ill demeanor to secular Princes, that the Heathen should count them disobedient or seditious workers against the States of the world. \fq ¹ \ft Obedience to temporal princes. \fq ² \ft \+it Ro. 13.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 2.13 \fq To every human creature. \ft So he calleth the temporal Magistrate elected by the people, or holding their sovereignty by birth & carnal propagation, ordained for the worldly wealth, peace, and prosperity of the subjects: to put a difference betwixt that human Superiority, and the spiritual Rulers and regiment, guiding and governing the people to an higher end, and instituted ¹ by God himself immediately. for Christ did expressly constitute the form of regiment used ever since in the Church. He made one the chief, placing Peter in the Supremacy: he called the Apostles and Disciples, giving them their several authorities. Afterward ² God guided the lot for choice of S. Matthias in Judas' place: and the Holy Ghost expressly and namely severed and chose Paul and Barnabas unto their Apostolical function: and generally the Apostle saith of all spiritual Rulers, \+it The Holy Ghost hath placed you to rule the Church of God\+it* ³. And although all power be of God, and kings rule by him, yet that is no otherwise, but by his ordinary concurrence, and providence, whereby he procureth the earthly commodity or wealth of men, by maintaining of due superiority and subjection one towards another, and by giving power to the people and Common wealth to choose to themselves some kind or form of Regiment, under which they be content to live for their preservation in peace and tranquility. But Spiritual superiority is far more excellent, as in more excellent sort depending, not of man's ordinance, election, or (as this Apostle speaketh) creation, but of the Holy Ghost, who is always resident in the Church (which is Christ's body mystical, and therefore another manner of Commonwealth than the earthly) concurring in singular sort to the creation of all necessary Officers in the said Church, even to the world's end, as S. Paul writeth to the Ephesians ⁴. \fq ¹ \ft God instituted the Spiritual government in more excellent manner than the temporal. \fq ² \ft \+it Act. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 20.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Eph. 4.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 2.13 \ft Lest therefore the people, being then in so precise sort always warned of the excellency of their Spiritual governors ¹ and of their obedience toward them, might neglect their duties to Temporal Magistrates, specially being infidels, and many times tyrants and persecutors of the faith, as Nero and other were then: therefore S. Peter here warneth them to be subject, for their bodies and goods and other temporal things, even to the worldly Princes both infidels and Christians, whom he calleth human creatures. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Hebr. 13.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 2.13 \fq To the king as excelling. \ft Some simple heretics & others also not unlearned, at the beginning, for lack of better places, would have proved by this, that the king was head of the Church, and above all Spiritual rulers: and to make it sound better that way, they falsely translate ¹ it. \+it To the king as to the chief head\+it*. in the Bible of the year 1562. But it is evident that he calleth the king, the precellent or more excellent, ² in respect of his Vicegerents which he calleth Dukes or Governors that be at his appointment: and not in respect of Popes, Bishops, or Priests, as they have the rule of men's souls: who could not in that charge be under such Kings or Emperors as the Apostle speaketh of: no more than the kings or Emperors then, could be heads of the Church, being Heathen men and no members thereof, much less the chief members. See a notable place in S. Ignatius ep. ad Smyrnenses, where he exhorteth them first to honour God, next the Bishop, & then the king. ³ This is an invincible demonstration, that this text maketh not for any spiritual claim of earthly kings, because it giveth no more to any Prince than may and ought to be done and granted to a Heathen Magistrate. Neither is there any thing in all the new Testament that proveth the Prince to be head or chief governor of the Church in spiritual or Ecclesiastical causes, more than it proveth any heathen Emperor of Rome to have been. For they were bound in temporal things to obey the heathen being lawful kings, to be subject to them even for conscience, to keep their temporal laws, to pay them tribute, to pray for them, and to do all other natural duties: and more no scriptures bind us to do to Christian kings. \fq ¹ \ft Heret. translation. \fq ² \ft The King's excellency of power is in respect of the nobility and law magistrates under him. \fq ³ \ft Christian Princes have no more right to be supreme heads in spiritual causes, than the Heathen.\ef*
\v 14 or to rulers as sent by him to the revenge of malefactors, but to the praise of the good:
\v 15 for so is the will of God, that doing well you may make the ignorance of unwise men to be dumb.
\v 16 as free, and not as having the freedom for a cloak of malice, but as the servants of God. \ef - \fr 2.16 \fq Not as having. \ft There were some Libertines in those days, as there be now, that under pretence of liberty of the Gospel, ¹ sought to be free from subjection and laws of men as now under the like wicked pretence, Heretics refuse to obey their spiritual rulers and to observe their laws. \fq ¹ \ft Libertines.\ef*
\v 17 Honour all men. \f 1 \fr 2.17 \ft c In this speech is often commended the unity of all Christians among themselves.\f* Love the fraternity. Fear God. Honour the king.
\v 18 Servants be subject in all fear to your masters, not only to the good & modest, but also to the wayward. \ef - \fr 2.18 \fq But also the wayward. \ft ¹ The Wiclefists and their followers in these days, sometimes to move the people unto sedition, hold and teach that masters and magistrates lose their authority over their servants and subjects, if they be once in deadly sin, and that the people in that case need not in conscience obey them. Which is a pernicious and false doctrine, as is plain by this place, where we be expressly commanded to obey even the ill conditioned. Which must be always understood, if they command nothing against God. for then this rule is ever to be followed. \+it We must obey God rather than men\+it*. Act. 5. 29. \fq ¹ \ft Deadly sins of Princes or Superiors exempt not the subjects from obedience, as Wicleffe held.\ef*
\v 19 For this is thank, if for conscience of God a man sustain sorrows, suffering unjustly.
\v 20 For what glory is it, if sinning, and buffeted you suffer? But if doing well you sustain patiently, this is thank before God.
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 2.21 \ft The Epistle upon the 2. Sunday after Easter.\f* For unto this are you called: because Christ also suffered for \f 2 \fr 2.21 \fq us \ft you\f* us, leaving \f 3 \fr 2.21 \fq you \ft \+it us\+it*\f* you an example that you may follow his steps.
\v 22 \x - \xt Esa. 53, 9.\x* \it Who did no sin, neither was guile found in his mouth.\it*
\v 23 Who when he was reviled, \f 1 \fr 2.23 \ft c ὀυκ ἀντελοιδόρει.\f* did not revile: when he suffered, he threatened not: but delivered himself to him that judged him unjustly.
\v 24 Who himself \x - \xt Esa. 53, 4. Mt. 8, 17.\x* bare our sins in his body upon the tree: that dead to sins, we may live to justice. By whose stripes you are healed.
\v 25 For you were as sheep straying: but you be converted now to the Pastor & Bishop of your souls.
\c 3
\cd The duty of wives and husbands to each other. 9. None to do or speak evil by their persecutors, 15. but to answer them always with modesty, and specially with innocency, after the example of Christ most innocent: whose body though they killed, yet his soul lived and preached afterward to the souls in Hell (namely to those in the time of Noe's flood being a figure of our Baptism) rose again, and ascended.
\p
\v 1 IN \x - \xt Eph. 5, 28. Col. 3, 18.\x* like manner also \f 2 \fr 3.1 \ft How wives should behave themselves toward their husbands.\f* let the women be subject to their husbands: that if any believe not the word, by the conversation of the women without the word they may be won,
\v 2 considering your chaste conversation in fear.
\v 3 \x - \xt 1. Tim. 2, 9.\x*\f 2 \fr 3.3 \ft Against the proud, curious and costly attire of women, wherein this ill time of our exceedeth.\f* Whose trimming let it not be outwardly the plaiting of hair, or laying on gold round about, or of putting on vestures:
\v 4 but the man of the heart that is hidden, in the incorruptibility of a quiet and a modest spirit, which is rich in the sight of God.
\v 5 For so sometime the holy women also that trusted in God, adorned themselves, subject to their own husbands.
\v 6 As \x - \xt Gen. 18.\x* Sara obeyed Abraham, calling him lord: \f 2 \fr 3.6 \ft \+it c ἧς\+it*\f* whose daughters you are, doing well, and not fearing any perturbation.
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 3.7 \ft How husbands should behave themselves toward their wives.\f* Husbands likewise, dwelling with them according to knowledge, as unto the weaker feminine vessel imparting honour, as it were to the coheirs also of the grace of life: that your prayers be not hindered.
\v 8 \f 1 \fr 3.8 \ft The Epistle upon the 5. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* And \f 2 \fr 3.8 \fq in fine \ft \+it in faith\+it*\f* in fine all of one mind, having compassion, lovers of the fraternity, merciful, modest, humble.
\v 9 \x - \xt Pro. 17, 13. Mat. 5, 44.\x* not rendering evil for evil, nor curse for curse: but contrarywise, blessing: for unto this are you called, that you may by inheritance possess a benediction.
\v 10 \x - \xt Ps. 33, 13.\x* \it For he that will love life, and see good days, let him refrain his tongue from evil, & his lips that they speak not guile.\it*
\v 11 \it Let him decline from evil, and do good: let him inquire peace, & follow it:\it*
\v 12 \it because the eyes of our Lord are upon the just, and his ears unto their prayers: but the countenance of our Lord upon them that do evil things.\it*
\v 13 And who is he that can hurt you, if you be emulators of good?
\v 14 But \x - \xt Mat. 5, 10.\x* & if you suffer ought for justice, blessed are ye. And the fear of them fear ye not, & be not troubled.
\v 15 But sanctify our Lord Christ in your hearts, ready always to satisfy every one that asketh you a reason of that hope which is in you:
\v 16 but with modesty and fear, having a good conscience: that in that which they speak ill of you, they may be confounded which calumniate your good conversation in Christ.
\v 17 For it is better to suffer as doing well (if the will of God will have it so) than doing ill.
\v 18 \f 1 \fr 3.18 \ft The Epistle upon Friday in Easter week.\f* Because Christ also died once for our sins, the just for the unjust: that he might offer us to God, mortified certes in flesh, but quickened in spirit.
\v 19 In the which spirit coming he preached to \f 1 \fr 3.19 \fq them \ft \+it those spirits\+it*\f* them also that were in prison: \ef - \fr 3.19 \fq To them that were in prison. \ft S. Augustine in his 99 Epistle \+it in principio\+it*, confesseth this place to be exceeding hard to understand, & to have many difficulties which he could never explicate to his own satisfaction. Yet unto Heretics this and all other texts be easy, not doubting but that is the sense which themselves imagine, whatsoever other men deem thereof. ¹ S. Augustine only findeth himself sure of this, that Christ's descending into Hell in soul after his death, is plainly proved hereby. Which thing he declareth there, to be conformable to diverse other express words of holy Writ, and namely to this same Apostle's sermon Act. 2. And at length he concludeth thus, \+it Quis ergo nisi infidelis negaverit fuisse apud inferos Christum?\+it* that is, \+it Therefore who but an infidel, will deny that Christ was in Hell?\+it* Calvin ² then (you see) with all his followers are infidels, who in stead of this descending of Christ in soul after his death, have invented another desperate kind of Christ's being in Hell, when he was yet alive on the Cross. S. Athanasius also in his epistle cited by S. Epiphanius \+it her. 77 in principio\+it*, and in his book \+it de incarnatione Verbi propius initio\+it*, S. Cyril \+it de recta fide fide ad Theodosium\+it*, Oecumenius, and diverse others upon this place, prove Christ's descending to Hell. As they likewise declare upon the words following, that he preached to the spirits or souls of men detained in Hell or in Prison. \fq ¹ \ft Christ in soul descended unto hell, whilst his body lay in the grave. \fq ² \ft The Calvinists denying the same, are (by S. Augustine's judgement) infidels.\ef* \ef - \fr 3.19 \ft But whether this word \+it Prison\+it* or \+it Hell\+it* be meant of the inferior place of the damned, or of \+it Limbus patrum\+it* called Abraham's bosom, or some other place of temporal chastisement: and, to whom he preached there, and who by his preaching or presence there were delivered, and who they were that are called, \+it Incredulous in the days of Noe\+it*: ¹ all these thins S. Augustine calleth great profundities, confessing himself to be unable to reach unto it: only holding fast and assured this article of our faith, that he delivered none deputed to damnation in the lowest hell, and yet not doubting but that he released diverse out of places of pains there. Which cannot be out of any other place than Purgatory ². See the said Epistle, where also he insinuateth other expositions for explication of the manifold difficulties of this hard text, which were too long to rehearse, our special purpose being only to note briefly the things that touch the controversies of this time. \fq ¹ \ft Certain difficulties whereof S. Augustine doubteth. \fq ² \ft Purgatory.\ef*
\v 20 which had been incredulous sometime, \x - \xt Gen. 6. Mat. 24.\x* when they expected the patience of God in the days of Noe, when the ark was a building: in the which, few, that is, \x - \xt Gen. 7, 7.\x* eight souls were saved by water. \ef - \fr 3.20 \fq Incredulous sometime. \ft ¹ They that take the former words, of Christ's descending to Hell, and delivering certain there detained, do expound this, not of such as died in their infidelity or without all faith in God. for such were not delivered: but either of some that once were incredulous, and afterward repented before their death: or rather and specially of such as otherwise were faithful, but yet trusted not Noe's preaching by his work and word, that God would destroy the world by water. Who yet being otherwise good men, when the matter came to pass, were sorry for their error, and died by the flood corporally, but yet in state of salvation. and being chastised for their fault in the next life, were delivered by Christ's descending thither. and not they only, but all others in the like condition. For the Apostle giveth these of Noe's time but for an example. \fq ¹ \ft What were the incredulous persons of whom the Apostle here speaketh.\ef*
\v 21 Whereunto Baptism being of the like form now saveth \f 1 \fr 3.21 \fq you \ft \+it us\+it*\f* you also: not the laying away of the filth of the flesh, but the examination of a good conscience toward God by the resurrection of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ. \ef - \fr 3.21 \fq Of the like form. \ft ¹ The water bearing up the Ark from sinking, and the persons in it from drowning, was a figure of Baptism, that likewise saveth the worthy receivers from everlasting perishing. \+it As Noe\+it* (saith S. Augustine) \+it with his, was delivered by the water and the wood, so the family of Christ by Baptism signed with Christ's Passion of the Cross. Li. 12. Cont. Faustum. c. 141\+it*. Again he saith ², that as the water saved none out of the Ark, but was rather their destruction: so the Sacrament of Baptism received out of the Catholic Church at Heretic's or Schismatic's hands, though it be the same water and Sacrament that the Catholic Church hath, yet profiteth none to salvation, but rather worketh their perdition. ³ Which yet is not meant in case of extreme necessity, when the party should die without the said Sacrament, except he took it at the Heretic's or Schismatic's hand. Neither is it meant in the case of infants, to whom the Sacrament is cause of salvation, they being in no fault for receiving it at the hands of the unfaithful. though their parents and friends that offer them unto such to be baptized, be in no small fault. S. Hierom to Damasus Pope of Rome ⁴, compareth that See to the Ark, & them that communicate with it, to them that were saved in the Ark: all other Schismatics and Heretics, to the rest that were drowned. \fq ¹ \ft Noe's Ark & the water, a figure of Christ's Cross & Baptism. \fq ² \ft \+it Ibid. c. 17.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Baptism received of Heretics or Schismatics, when damnable, when not. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Ep. 57.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 3.21 \fq The examination of a good conscience. \ft ¹ The Apostle seemeth to allude here to the very form of Catholic Baptism, containing certain interrogatories and solemn promises made of the articles of the Christian faith, and of good life, and of renouncing Satan and all his pomps and works. which (no doubt) howsoever the Calvinists esteem of them, are the very Apostolic ceremonies used in the ministration of this Sacrament. \+it See S. Denys in fine Ec. hierarchia. S. Cyril li. 12 in 10. c. 64. S. Augustine ep. 23. S. Basil de Sp. Sancto c. 12 and 15. S. Ambrose de ijs qui mysterijs initiantur c. 2. 3. 4.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The ceremonies of Baptism, namely \+it Abrenuntio. &c.\+it*\ef*
\v 22 Who is on the right hand of God, swallowing death, that we might be made heirs of life everlasting: being gone into Heaven, Angels and Potentates and Powers subjected to him.
\c 4
\cd That they arm themselves to sin no more after Baptism, against the tentations of the Heathen, considering that the general end now approacheth. 8. specially toward their even-Christians to shew their charity, hospitality, and grace, doing all to the glory of God. 12. And as for being persecuted because they are Christians, to rejoice, considering the reward that they shall have with Christ, and damnation that they avoid thereby.
\p
\v 1 \sc Christ\sc* therefore having suffered in the flesh, be you also armed with the same cogitation. Because he that hath suffered in the flesh, hath ceased from sins:
\v 2 that now not after the desires of men, but according to the will of God he live the rest of his time in the flesh.
\v 3 For the time past sufficeth (to accomplish the will of the Gentiles) them that have walked in riotousness, desires, excess of wine, banquetings, potations, and unlawful services of idols.
\v 4 Wherein they marvel blaspheming, you not concurring into the same confusion of riotousness.
\v 5 Who shall render account to him, which is ready to judge the living and the dead.
\v 6 For, for this cause also was \f 1 \fr 4.6 \ft It hath the same difficulty and sense that the other like words have before, \+it Chap. 3.\+it* See the annotation there \+it v. 19.\+it* and S. Aug. \+it ep. 69.\+it* & Oecumenius \+it upon this place.\+it*\f* it evangelized to the dead: that they may be judged indeed according to men, in the flesh: but may live according to God in the Spirit.
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 4.7 \ft The Epistle upon Sunday next after the Ascension.\f* And the end of all \f 2 \fr 4.7 \fq shall approach \ft \+it is at hand.\+it*\f* shall approach. Be wise therefore, and watch in prayers.
\v 8 But before all things, having mutual charity continual among yourselves: because \x - \xt Prov. 10.\x* charity covereth the multitude of sins. \ef - \fr 4.8 \fq Charity covereth. \ft ¹ Faith only cannot justify, seeing that charity also doth cause remission of sins. And saying charity, he meaneth love and charitable works toward our neighbours, unto which works ² of mercy the Scriptures do specially attribute the force to extinguish all sins. See S. Augustine \+it c. 69 Enchiridij and tract. 1. in ep. 1. Io. c. 1.\+it* and venerable Bede \+it upon this place\+it*. And in the like sense the holy Scriptures commonly commend unto us alms and deeds of mercy for redemption of our sins. \+it Proverb. c. 10. Ecclesiastici 12. v. 2. Danielis c. 4 v. 24.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Not only faith. \fq ² \ft Works of mercy.\ef*
\v 9 \x - \xt Ro. 12, Heb. 13.\x* Using hospitality one toward another without murmuring.
\v 10 \x - \xt Ro. 12, 6.\x* Every one as he hath received grace, ministering the same one toward another: as good dispensers of the manifold grace of God.
\v 11 If any man speak, as the words of God. If any man minister, as of the power, which God administereth. That in all things God may be honoured by \sc Jesus\sc* Christ: to whom is glory & empire for ever and ever. Amen.
\v 12 My dearest, think it not strange in the fervour which is to you for a tentation, as though some new thing happened to you:
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 4.13 \ft The Epistle for some Martyrs.\f* But communicating with the passions of Christ, be glad, that in the revelation also of his glory you may be glad rejoicing.
\v 14 \x - \xt Mt. 5, 11.\x* If you be reviled in the name of Christ, you shall be blessed: because that which is of the honour, glory, and virtue of God, and the Spirit which is his, shall rest upon you.
\v 15 But let none of you suffer as a murderer, or a thief, or a railer, or a coveter of other men's things.
\v 16 But if as a Christian, let him not be ashamed, but let him glorify God in this name.
\v 17 For \x - \xt Hier. 25, 29.\x* the time is that judgement begin of the house of God. And if first of us, what shall be the end of them that believe not the Gospel of God? \ef - \fr 4.17 \fq That judgement begin. \ft ¹ In this time of the new Testament, the faithful and all those that mean to live godly (specially of the Clergy) must first and principally be subject to God's chastisement and temporal afflictions, which are here called judgement. Which the Apostle recordeth for the comfort and confirmation of the Catholic Christians, who were at the time of the writing hereof, exceedingly persecuted by the heathen Princes & people. \fq ¹ \ft The better men most afflicted in this life.\ef*
\v 18 And \x - \xt Prov. 11, 31.\x* if the just man shall scarce be saved, where shall the impious & sinner appear? \ef - \fr 4.18 \fq If the just. \ft ¹ Note that a man dying just and in the favour of God, can afterward be in doubt of his salvation, or may be rejected of God: but that the just being both in this life subject to assaults, tentations, troubles, and dangers of falling from God and losing their state of justice, & also oftentimes to make a strait count, & to be temporally chastised in the next life, cannot be saved without great watch, fear, and trembling, and much labouring and chastisement. ² And this is far contrary to the Protestants' doctrine, that putteth no justice but in faith alone, maketh none just indeed and in truth, teacheth men to be so secure and assured of their salvation, that he that hath lived wickedly all his life, if he only have their faith at his death, that is, if he believe steadfastly that he is one of the elect, he shall be as sure of his salvation immediately after his departure, as the best liver in the world. \fq ¹ \ft The just man himself is hardly saved. \fq ² \ft Against the vain security of only faith.\ef*
\v 19 Therefore they also that suffer according to the will of God, let them commend their souls to the faithful Creator, in good deeds.
\c 5
\cd He exhorteth Priests to feed their flocks, only for God's sake and reward of heaven, without all lordliness. 5. the lay to obey: all to be humble one towards another. 8. to be constant in the Catho. faith, considering it is not man, but that lion the Devil that persecuteth them, 9. as he doth the whole Church also, & that God will after a while make them secure in heaven.
\p
\v 1 \sc The\sc* \f 1 \fr 5.1 \ft The Epistle for S. Apollinaris. \+it Iul. 23.\+it*\f* seniors therefore that are among you, I beseech, myself a fellow senior with them & a witness of the Passions of Christ, who am also partaker of the glory which is to be revealed in time to come: \ef - \fr 5.1 \fq Seniors. \ft ¹ Though the Latin, \+it Senior\+it*, be not appropriated to Holy Order by use of speech, neither in the Latin nor in our language: yet it is plain that the Greek word \+it Presbyter\+it*, which the Apostle here useth, is here also (as commonly in other places of the new Testament) a word of Ecclesiastical office, and not of age, and is as much to say as \+it Priest\+it* or \+it Bishop\+it*. For the Apostle himself being of that order, speaketh (as by his words it is plain) to such as had charge of souls, saying, \+it Fees the flock of God which is among you\+it*. Because we follow the vulgar Latin translation, we say \+it Seniors and Senior\+it*: whereas otherwise we might and should say according to the Greek, \+it The Priests therefore I beseech, myself a fellow-priest with them\+it*. So doth S. Hierom read ( \+it Presbyteros compresbyter\+it* ) and expound ep. 85. So translateth Erasmus, and Beza himself. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Senior\+it* in the vulgar translation is often Priest or Bishop. \+it See Act. 15.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 feed the flock of God which is among you \f 1 \fr 5.2 \ft \+it c ἐπισκοποῦντες.\+it*\f* providing not by constraint, but willingly according to God: \f 2 \fr 5.2 \ft Desire of lucre, or to exercise holy functions for gain, is a filthy fault in the Clergy, and therefore much to be avoided.\f* neither for filthy lucre sake, but voluntarily:
\v 3 neither as over-ruling the Clergy, but made examples of the flock from the heart. \ef - \fr 5.3 \fq Overruling. \ft ¹ Not superiority, preeminence, sovereignty, or rule on the one side, nor obedience, subjection, and inferiority on the other side, be forbidden in the Clergy: but tyranny, pride, and ambitious domination be forbidden, and humility, meekness, moderation are commended in Ecclesiastical Officers. The Greek word here of ruling or overruling ², being the same that our Saviour useth in the Gospel ³ of the tyrannical rule of secular Heathen Princes, saying to his Apostles, that it shall not be so among them: according as here the prince of the Apostles teacheth his brethren the Ecclesiastical rulers. \fq ¹ \ft Not Superiority but tyranny and lordliness is forbidden in the Clergy. \fq ² \ft \+it κατακυριεύοντες.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Mat. 20. v. 25.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 5.3 \fq The Clergy. \ft ¹ Some of the English new translations turn it corruptly, \+it Parishes\+it*: others, \+it heritages\+it*: both, to avoid the most known, true, and common word in all Christian languages, to wit, ² \+it Clergy\+it*, a word, by use of all antiquity, & agreeably to the holy Scriptures, made proper to the Spiritualty or Clergy. though in another more vulgar acception it may agree to all Christ's chosen heritage, as well of lay people as Priests, which the Protestants had rather follow, because they will have no difference between the laity & the Clergy. But the holy fathers far otherwise even from the beginning. Whereof see S. Cyprian ep. 4. 5. 6. &c. And S. Hierom ep. 2 to Nepotianus c. 5. where he interpreteth this word. \+it Therefore\+it* (saith he) \+it Clericus\+it*, that is, \+it a Clergy man, which serveth the Church of Christ, let him first interpret his name, and the signification of the name being declared, let him endeavour to be that which he is called\+it*. If \+it κλῆρος\+it* (Clerus) \+it in Greek, be called in Latin, Sors, therefore are they called Clerici, that is, Clergy men, because they are of the lot of our Lord, or because our Lord himself is the lot or portion of Clergy men &c.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Heret. translation. \fq ² \ft The name of Clergy and Clerk.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.3 \ft Which calling no doubt was taken out of the holy Scriptures, \+it Numer. 18.\+it* and \+it Deutero. 18.\+it* Where God is called the inheritance ¹, lot, and portion of the Priests and Levites: and now when men be made the Clergy, they say, \+it Dominus pars hæreditatis meæ\+it*. That is, \+it Our Lord is the portion of mine inheritance\+it*. but specially out of the new Testament, Act. 1, 17. 25. and 8, 21. Where the lot or office of the Ecclesiastical ministry is called by this word \+it κλῆρος\+it*, Clerus. See in Venerable Bede the causes why this holy state being severed by name from the Laity, doth wear also a crown ² on their head for distinction. \+it Liv. 5. hist. Angl. c. 22.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it κληρονομια. κλῆρος. μερὶς.\+it* \fq ² \ft Priests' crowns.\ef*
\v 4 And when the Prince of pastors shall appear, you shall receive the incorruptible crown of glory. \ef - \fr 5.4 \fq Crown of glory. \ft ¹ As life everlasting shall be the reward of all the just, so the preachers & Pastors that do well, for their going shall have that reward in a more excellent degree, expressed here by these words, \+it Crown of glory\+it*. according to the saying of Daniel c. 12. \+it They that sleep in the dust of the earth, shall awake, one sort to life everlasting, others to everlasting rebuke. but such as be learned, shall shine as the brightness of the firmament: and such as instruct many to justice, shall be as stars, during all eternity\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The heavenly crown of Doctors and preachers.\ef*
\v 5 In like manner ye young men be subject to the seniors. And do ye all insinuate humility one to another, because \it God resisteth the proud, and to the humble he giveth grace.\it*
\v 6 \x - \xt Prov. 3. Iac. 4. Iac. 4. Psa. 54. Mt. 6, 25.\x*\f 2 \fr 5.6 \ft The Epistle upon the 3. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* Be ye humbled therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in the time of visitation:
\v 7 casting all your carefulness upon him, because he hath care of you.
\v 8 Be sober and watch: because your adversary the devil as a roaring lion goeth about, seeking whom he may devour.
\v 9 Whom resist ye, strong in faith: knowing that the self-same affliction is made to that your fraternity which is in the world.
\v 10 But the God of all grace, which hath called us unto his eternal glory in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*, he will perfit you having suffered a little, and confirm, and stablish you.
\v 11 To him be glory and empire for ever and ever. Amen.
\v 12 By Sylvanus, a faithful brother to you, as I think, I have briefly written: beseeching & testifying that this is the true grace of God, wherein you stand.
\v 13 The Church saluteth you, that is in Babylon, co-elect: and Mark my son. \ef - \fr 5.13 \fq That is in Babylon. \ft ¹ The Protestants shew themselves here (as in all places where any controversy is, or that maketh against them) to be most unhonest and partial handlers of God's word. The ancient fathers, namely S. Hierom \+it in Catalogo de Scriptoribus Ecclesiasticis, verbo Marcus\+it*: Eusebius \+it li. 2. c. 14. hist. Oecumenius\+it* upon this place: and many more agree, that Rome is meant by the word Babylon, here also as in the 16 and 17 of the Apocalypse: saying plainly, that S. Peter wrote this Epistle at Rome, ² which is called Babylon for the resemblance it had to Babylon that great city in Chaldæa (where the Jews were captives) for magnificence, Monarchy, resort and confusion of all peoples and tongues, and for that it was before Christ and long after, the seat of all Ethnic superstition & idolatry, & the slaughter house of the Apostles & other Christian men, the Heathen Emperors then keeping their chief residence there. See S. Leo \+it Ser. 1 in Nati. Petri & Pauli\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft S. Peter writeth from Babylon, that is, Rome. \fq ² \ft Why Rome was called Babylon.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.13 \ft This being most plain, and consonant to that which followeth of S. Mark, whom all the Ecclesiastical histories agree to have been Peter's scholar at Rome, and that he there wrote his Gospel: ¹ yet our Adversaries fearing hereby the sequel of Peter's or the Pope's supremacy at Rome, deny that ever he was there, or that this Epistle was written there, or that Babylon doth here signify Rome: but they say that Peter wrote this Epistle at Babylon in Chaldæa, though they never read either in Scriptures or other holy or profane history, that this Apostle was ever in that town. but see their shameless partiality. Here Babylon (say they) is not taken for Rome, because it would follow that Peter was at Rome &c. but in the Apocalypse where all evil is spoken of Babylon, there they will have it signify nothing else but Rome, and the Roman Church also, not (as the fathers interpret it) the temporal state of the Heathen Empire there. So do they follow in every word no other thing but the advantage of their own heresy. See the Annotation upon the last of the Romans v. 16. and upon the 17 of the Apocalypse. v. 5. ² And as for their wrangling upon the supputation of the time of his going thither, and the number of years that he was there, & the diversity that seemeth to be in the Ecclesiastical writers concerning the same, read B. Fisher and other that substantially answer all such cavils. And if such contentious reasoning might take place, we should hardly believe the principal things recorded either in Ecclesiastical histories, or in the Scriptures themselves. Concerning the time of Christ's fleeing into Ægypt, of the coming of the Sages to adore him, yea of the year of his age, & time of his death, all ancient writers do not agree. and concerning the day of his last supper and institution of the holy Sacrament, there is diversity of opinions. Shall we therefore infer that he never died, and that the other things never were? ³ Can the Heretics accord all the histories that seem even in holy Scripture to have contradiction? Can they tell us certainly, when David first came to Saul, and the like? Doubt they whether the world was ever created, because the count of the years is diverse? Do they not believe that Paradise ever was, because no man knoweth where it is? and such other like things infinite to rehearse? Which when they were done, were plain and known things in the world: and now for us to call them to an account, after so many years, ages, and worlds, is but sophistication and plain infidelity. And this sect of the Protestants standing only upon destruction, and negatives, & dealing with our religion even as Iulian, Porphyrie, and Lucian did, it is an easy thing for them to bestow their time in picking of quarrels. \fq ¹ \ft The Protestants will have Babylon to signify Rome in other places but not here. \fq ² \ft The Protestants wrangle about the time of Peter's being at Rome. \fq ³ \ft Many things most true (even in the Scriptures) are not agreed upon concerning the time.\ef*
\v 14 \x - \xt Ro. 16, 16. 1. Cor. 16, 20. 2. Cor. 13, 12.\x* Salute one another in a holy kiss. Grace be to all you which are in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*. Amen.
